  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nExpansive high pressure will drift off the mid Atlantic coastal \nregion providing the southern Great Lakes with deep southerly flow \nin the next couple of days. With 850mb temperatures climbing into \nthe lower teens with warm air advection, expecting high temperatures \n10-20 degrees above normal for Tuesday and Wednesday, possibly \npushing record highs in places Tuesday. Wednesday should lag a bit \nwith the potential introduction of showers into the region ahead of \nthe cold front marching in from the west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDeep upper low will push from the southern high plains towards the \nupper Mississippi Valley, driving a Pacific cold front west to east \ninto the CWA. Line of showers moves into the western zones after 15Z \nWednesday. Looking at the low level f-gen for this system, it \nappears the system may turn weakly frontolytic as it moves through \nthe CWA Wednesday, with the line possibly becoming more scattered as \nit does so. This would correlate with the occluding nature of the \nsurface and upper level low pressure systems, but still expect \nprecipitation for all zones of the CWA and will need to watch for \nthe development of surface waves along the cold front. Temperatures \nback down towards normal for the end of the week and into the \nweekend with continued upper level troughing over the CWA and off \nand on rain chances during this time as well.",
